# Apache Airflow Orchestration Best Practices

## DAG Design and Structure
- MUST use clear, descriptive DAG IDs with 'ecommerce_' prefix
- MUST implement proper task dependencies using bitshift operators (>> and <<)
- MUST use appropriate task groups for logical organization
- MUST implement idempotent tasks that can be safely re-run
- MUST use XComs sparingly and prefer file-based data exchange

## Task Implementation
- MUST implement proper error handling with try-catch blocks
- MUST use appropriate retry policies with exponential backoff
- MUST implement task timeouts to prevent hanging workflows
- MUST use task-specific execution timeouts
- MUST implement graceful failure handling with clear error messages

## Scheduling and Triggers
- MUST use appropriate schedule intervals based on data freshness requirements
- MUST implement data-driven scheduling when possible
- MUST use external task sensors for cross-DAG dependencies
- MUST implement proper timezone handling for global operations
- MUST use catchup=False for continuous processing DAGs

## Resource Management
- MUST configure appropriate pool sizes for resource-constrained environments
- MUST implement proper memory and CPU limits for tasks
- MUST use task-level resource allocation when available
- MUST implement proper cleanup of temporary resources
- MUST monitor task execution times and resource usage

## Data Quality and Monitoring
- MUST implement data quality checks as separate tasks
- MUST use SLA monitoring for critical data pipelines
- MUST implement proper alerting for task failures
- MUST log data lineage and processing metrics
- MUST implement data validation before downstream processing

## Configuration and Environment
- MUST externalize configuration using Airflow Variables or Connections
- MUST implement environment-specific configurations
- MUST use proper secrets management for sensitive data
- MUST implement proper logging configuration
- MUST use DAG-level default arguments for consistency

## Performance Optimization
- MUST implement parallel processing where possible
- MUST use appropriate concurrency settings
- MUST implement proper task scheduling to avoid resource contention
- MUST use subDAGs or TaskGroups for complex workflows
- MUST implement proper cleanup of old DAG runs and logs